      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section id="outcome" tone="paper" padding="default" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 04</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">What shipping it changed</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5">
            <Text size="lg" tone="muted">
              The app now reads as the same caliber product it always was. The
              behind-the-scenes pipeline, the client-specific rules, the connections to
              outside recruiting systems, and the way each client's data stays separate
              from every other client's: none of that changed. A user who logged in
              the day the new version went live saw their data, their filters, their
              scroll position, and their workflows intact. They just saw them inside a
              shell that looks like it belongs in 2026.
            </Text>
            <Text size="lg" tone="muted">
              That shift paid off on both sides of the business. Operators who spent every
              working day inside the tool stopped mentally filtering out visual noise: buttons,
              forms, tables, and labels finally behaved the same from screen to screen, which
              made the product feel deliberate and trustworthy rather than patched together.
              Leadership stopped apologizing for the admin app in investor demos. The audit is
              what made both of those possible:
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">nothing got fixed until every inconsistency
              had been written down and traced to its cause.</strong>
            </Text>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
            {outcomeStats.map((s) => (
              <div class="border-t border-line pt-5">
                <Icon name={s.icon} size={24} class="mb-3 text-ink-muted" />
                <Heading level={3} visual="h4" tone="ink" class="mb-1">{s.title}</Heading>
                <Text size="sm" tone="muted">{s.body}</Text>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12">
            <p class="mono-label mb-3">A detail worth calling out</p>
            <Heading level={3} visual="h3" tone="ink" class="mb-4">Aligning the app with the marketing site</Heading>
            <Text tone="muted" class="measure">
              Partway through Phase 2 I took the five official brand colors already
              defined on the public marketing website and pointed the admin app's
              internal color system at those same values instead of its own separate
              set. The underlying system for handling color didn't need to be
              restructured. I just swapped in the correct values and added three new
              ones, and every screen that already used that color system picked up the
              new look automatically. Two small details carried over on purpose:
              hovering over the main button changes it from orange to steel blue
              rather than just getting darker, and hovering over a link makes it go
              nearly black rather than lighter, both matching how the marketing site
              already behaved.
            </Text>

            <div class="mt-8">
              <SiteShot
                src="/images/projects/vmspark/vmspark-live.webp"
                alt="The VMSpark marketing site, the source of the five pinned brand colors the admin app's token layer was re-pointed at"
                href="https://vmspark.com/"
                label="vmspark.com"
                index="01"
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="my-12 border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
            <blockquote class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight text-ink leading-snug">
              Operators live in this shell all day. Investors see it for thirty seconds in a
              demo. The audit is what let me rebuild it without breaking what's underneath.
            </blockquote>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

